(2155608, 292854, 5112, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by BobsterLobster:</b></div><hr/><i> What''s the shutter speed and aperture? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Sorry, I thought I''d typed that in.  Set to Shutter priority at 1/30 sec.  Aperture went to F13 in this case.  I like 1/30 for panning, as it seems to work well for quite a variety of subjects.</td>', 'Originally posted by BobsterLobster: What''s the shutter speed and aperture?   Sorry, I thought I''d typed that in.  Set to Shutter priority at 1/30 sec.  Aperture went to F13 in this case.  I like 1/30 for panning, as it seems to work well for quite a variety of subjects.', '2006-02-15 22:44:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2155716, 292451, 52578, '<td valign="top">Yeah the driver has his front right tyre locked... if you look really closely you can see that he is turning slightly to the right already (next corner is a 90degree right) so the weight is transferred off the front right and thus that is the first one to lock. The car is still travelling at over 100kph at this point. The driver did not hit any walls, just went a bit wide at the apex of the corner.<br/> <br/>Like many mentioned my major gripe was having the car too near the top of the frame. Cutting off the back of the car is intentional because the back end makes it look really girlie. In this picture it would''ve worked though because the rear wheel would''ve been spinning and you could''ve seen the smoke moving around it... next frame had both wheels but crap sharpness.... but these were the only two frames of a car with it''s tyre properly locked after 3 days of shooting. I had lots of really cool panned shots but figured that the locked tyre adds a lot, and i think i was right.<br/> <br/>For those wondering about the title - i had submitted a different pic originally and my slugish internet connection resulted in the title not being updated to reflect the current image.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-17 06:14:19.</i></td>', 'Yeah the driver has his front right tyre locked... if you look really closely you can see that he is turning slightly to the right already (next corner is a 90degree right) so the weight is transferred off the front right and thus that is the first one to lock. The car is still travelling at over 100kph at this point. The driver did not hit any walls, just went a bit wide at the apex of the corner. Like many mentioned my major gripe was having the car too near the top of the frame. Cutting off the back of the car is intentional because the back end makes it look really girlie. In this picture it would''ve worked though because the rear wheel would''ve been spinning and you could''ve seen the smoke moving around it... next frame had both wheels but crap sharpness.... but these were the only two frames of a car with it''s tyre properly locked after 3 days of shooting. I had lots of really cool panned shots but figured that the locked tyre adds a lot, and i think i was right. For those wondering about the title - i had submitted a different pic originally and my slugish internet connection resulted in the title not being updated to reflect the current image.', '2006-02-15 23:32:38.000000', '2006-02-17 06:14:19.000000', 0),
